<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Attalus I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">269–<a href="/years/197-bce">197 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Attalus I Soter</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Attalus I Soter ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> from 241 to 197 BCE. After defeating the Galatians, he took the title of king, the first of his dynasty to do so, and celebrated the victory with monuments that included statues of dying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a>, beginning the partnership that made the Attalid kingdom Rome's chief friend in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/attalus-i.jpg" alt="Attalus I" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergey Sosnovskiy from Saint-Petersburg, Russia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_Hellenistic_ruler_(Attalus_I%3F)._Berlin,_Pergamon_Museum._(5256233048).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
